\section{General Instructions}

You can participate in this extra credit (EC) homework in two
different roles, depending on your performance in Quiz~1:
\begin{description}
\item[Solver:] If your score was 10 points or less in Quiz~1, you
can work on these questions and turn in your answers for grading.
You can obtain 8 EC points at most.

Your answers should be prepared in RStudio, using the combination
of R, \LaTeX, and Sweave (or knitr). Similar to our homeworks, you
need to submit the \verb|.Rnw| file and the \verb|.pdf| file produced on your side.
{\bf Do not include your names in any of these files. Only name the
file names according to the rules for our homeworks!}

\item[Grader:] If your score was between 11 and 20 points in Quiz~1, you
should still work on  these questions, but you receive points only
for grading the submissions from the Solvers.
You can obtain 4 EC points at most.

{\bf Please let me know by e--mail (by the due data) whether you want
to participate as Grader in this assignment.}

\end{description}

Everyone else can participate as an {\bf Advisor}. You should give hints,
either in person or when a question is asked in Canvas. You should
not provide a full answer! These EC questions are really intended
(and needed) for everyone who did not do so well in Quiz~1.

Depending on how many submissions I get from the Solvers, each Grader
may receive one or two such submissions. This will be anonymous,
i.e., the Graders won't know whom they are grading and the Solvers
won't know who were their Graders. 

The score for the Solvers will be determined by the Graders 
as the median of the reported scores obtained from
several Graders. Don't expect to obtain
full points this way.

I will only check the work of the Graders in the following way:
Graders start with the maximum number of 4 points. For each
falsely deducted point when grading a submission, the Grader
will be deducted 1/2 point. Moreover, if there was no point
deductation made by a Grader, but in case I would have made 
such a point deduction, the Grader also loses 1/2 point.
So, Graders are encouraged to grade carefully. The minimum
score for a Grader is 0 points. Graders will have
one week to finish grading.



