\section{Question 1 (3 Points)}

The Hermite polynomials are defined recursively by
\[
H_0 (x) = 1, ~~~ H_1 (x) = x, ~~~ H_{n+1} (x) = 
x H_n (x) - n H_{n-1} (x), \mbox{for } n \geq 1.
\]
Write a function called \verb|CalculateHermite| with arguments
\verb|n| and \verb|x|, where \verb|n| is a non--negative integer
and \verb|x| can be any numeric value. Do some minimal checks
that the values provided by the user meet these requirements.

Calculate the first 6 Hermite polynomials (for $x$ ranging
from $-5$ to $5$) and plot them on the same coordinate system.
Limit the range of the vertical axis to the interval $[-30, 30]$.

Compare your results with those shown at
\url{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermite_polynomials}.
Note that two families of Hermite polynomials exist.
Which of these is represented by our recursive formula above?
Do the necessary math or carefully compare your figure with 
the figures shown on the wikipedia page.



\section{Question 2 (3 Points)}

Recall that
\[
{n \choose k} = \frac{n!}{k! \cdot (n-k)!} = {n \choose n-k}.
\]
Instead of using factorials, we can use the following fast
calculations, as shown for two examples:
\[
{5 \choose 2} = \frac{5!}{2! \cdot 3!} = 
\frac{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1}{(1 \cdot 2) \cdot (3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1)} =
\frac{5 \cdot 4}{1 \cdot 2} = (((5 / 1) \cdot 4) / 2) = 10
\]
and
\[
{1000 \choose 999} = \frac{1000!}{999! \cdot 1!} = \frac{1000!}{1! \cdot 999!} =
\frac{1000 \cdot 999!}{1 \cdot 999!} =
\frac{1000}{1} = 1000 / 1 = 1000
\]
Write a function called \verb|myBinomialCoeff| with arguments
\verb|n| and \verb|k| that calculates Binomial coefficients
according to the principle above. 
Use a while--loop and alternate devision and multiplication
in the correct order!
Note that we won't have to calculate
large factorials this way, and, moreover, we never leave 
the domain of integer numbers during
our calculations (if we do this in the correct order).

Your function should check whether \verb|n| and \verb|k| both are valid 
non--negative integers. If not, return NA and issue a warning.

Test your function with these 2 examples above and add at least 5 more
test scenarios, two of which should include 0 for \verb|n| and/or \verb|k|.
Compare your results with those obtained from the build--in 
function \verb|choose|.



\section{Question 3 (2 Points)}

For the \verb|chip04.txt| data set from the {\it Graphics 2}
chapter of the lecture notes, recreate the plot in the 
upper left panel of the notes (showing all 4 lines, standardized
to 1 in 1975). Your version of this figure should resemble the printed
version from the lecture notes as closely as possible.


